{"type":"text","timestamp":1794380864365,"sessionID":"ses_e36a1605fffeQa463ZF52q8gD3","part":{"id":"prt_1c96f1b54001v8DnOMzkE6TF0p","messageID":"msg_1c96f0673001RFAtxsDQVO78gH","sessionID":"ses_e36a1605fffeQa463ZF52q8gD3","type":"text","text":"<think>The server was killed with the shell command. I need a way to run it detached, e.g. with setsid and redirecting stdin, then `disown`. The shell tool kills the process group on timeout.\n\nLet me use `setsid nohup ... &` and make sure the command returns immediately so the tool doesn't time out. The problem before: the `cat .run/app.log` was fine but the background job kept the shell alive → the tool waited 120s then killed.\n\nUse `setsid` to detach into a new session so the kill of the process group doesn't affect it. Let me try:\n\n`setsid python3 backend/app.py > .run/app.log 2>&1 < /dev/null & disown; sleep 2; curl ...`\n\nThe key: the shell tool waits for output EOF? If the background process inherits stdout... but we redirect to a file, so no. The issue may be that the tool waits for all children. Using setsid should detach it.\n\nLet me try that with a short timeout on the bash call.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794380864340,"end":1794380864360}}}
