{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794380983650,"sessionID":"ses_e36a1605fffeQa463ZF52q8gD3","part":{"id":"prt_1c970ed48001soDpsqnP70nlkr","messageID":"msg_1c970dc4f001UiJmbL6sQSurlW","sessionID":"ses_e36a1605fffeQa463ZF52q8gD3","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794380983651,"sessionID":"ses_e36a1605fffeQa463ZF52q8gD3","part":{"id":"prt_1c970ed4c0014W3LH6y6EnaScI","messageID":"msg_1c970dc4f001UiJmbL6sQSurlW","sessionID":"ses_e36a1605fffeQa463ZF52q8gD3","type":"text","text":"<think>The server seems to be the old process still (the log shows old entries and the health curl returned nothing?). Actually the curl output printed nothing before the log lines. Hmm, the first curl output is empty → the server may not be running (pkill killed it and the new one didn't start because the previous command timed out before starting it?).\n\nThe log tail shows old entries, meaning the log file wasn't overwritten → the new server didn't start (the command was killed at the `sleep 2` stage before... no, the setsid start happens before sleep 2).\n\nWait: the sequence was `pkill -f \"backend/app.py\"` — this may have killed the shell's own process? The bash command itself contains \"backend/app.py\" in its command line, so pkill -f matched the shell running the command and killed itself! That's why the command timed out weirdly and the server never restarted.\n\nLet me avoid pkill -f matching itself: use `pkill -f \"[b]ackend/app.py\"` trick or kill by port.\n\nLet me check if anything is listening and start fresh.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794380983628,"end":1794380983647}}}
