{"type":"text","timestamp":1794381017920,"sessionID":"ses_e36a1605fffeQa463ZF52q8gD3","part":{"id":"prt_1c971732b001iklTKu3xqkKJCC","messageID":"msg_1c9715daf001dDz8d3eD4Dhpcz","sessionID":"ses_e36a1605fffeQa463ZF52q8gD3","type":"text","text":"<think>The server isn't running and the log wasn't overwritten, meaning the setsid start didn't happen — the command was killed before reaching it? The tool reported a timeout after 25s, and output was \"(no output)\". Weird: the `pkill -f \"[b]ackend/app.py\"` pattern — the bracket trick should avoid matching itself, but the shell command line contains `[b]ackend/app.py` literally... pkill -f matches the full command line of the bash process, which contains \"[b]ackend/app.py\" — the regex `[b]ackend/app.py` matches the literal text \"backend/app.py\" but the bash command line contains \"[b]ackend/app.py\" which does NOT match the regex (since the regex requires \"backend/app.py\" literally). Hmm, but the bash command line ALSO contains \"python3 backend/app.py\" later in the same command line! Yes — the whole command string includes `python3 backend/app.py`, so pkill -f matched the shell itself and killed it.\n\nSo: run pkill in a separate call from the start command, or use a pattern that doesn't appear in the same command line. Let me kill by port using fuser/lsof, in itsown bash call.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794381017899,"end":1794381017916}}}
